</p>
<p>Black cannot have moved last, as there&#8217;s no square from which the king can legally have moved. The white pawns have not moved, the rook is entombed, and the knight would have had to move from b3, in which which case Black had no legal preceding move. So White just moved his king to c1. What was Black&#8217;s move before that? He can&#8217;t have moved his king, so he must have moved a black piece to c1, where it was captured by the white king. What piece? Only a knight could legally arrive on that square to be captured. So the white king captured a black knight on c1.</p>
